WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.860
 The Silent Surrender, Why Chrome and Edge Are Failing You

2
00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:08.580
 Chrome's Manifest V3 update has effectively killed the best ad blockers.

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.780
 Edge's telemetry pipeline is more aggressive than most users realize.

4
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.640
 Here is what changed and what to do about it.

5
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.880
 The browser is the most intimate piece of software most people use.

6
00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:23.560
 It is the lens through which they see the internet, the tool through which they conduct

7
00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:25.960
 their finances, their communications, their research.

8
00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:30.860
 For years, a combination of capable ad blockers and privacy-focused settings gave technically

9
00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:34.660
 aware users a reasonable degree of control over what was tracked and what was not.

10
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:36.020
 That era is ending.

11
00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:38.680
 The Manifest V3 Transition

12
00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:43.760
 Google's Chrome browser has completed its transition to Manifest V3, a new standard

13
00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:44.740
 for browser extensions.

14
00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:47.380
 The stated rationale was security and performance.

15
00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.960
 The practical effect has been to fundamentally limit the capabilities of content blocking

16
00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:51.480
 extensions.

17
00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:55.720
 The most powerful ad blockers, tools like uBlock Origin, relied on the older

18
00:00:55.960 --> 00:01:00.440
 Manifest V2 standards ability to intercept and modify network requests at a low level.

19
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:05.000
 Manifest V3 replaces this with a declarative system.

20
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:10.200
 Extensions must submit a list of rules in advance and Chrome decides whether to apply

21
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:10.480
 them.

22
00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:16.080
 The result is that dynamic, real-time blocking of sophisticated tracking scripts is no longer

23
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:17.240
 possible in Chrome.

24
00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:20.200
 Microsoft Edge's telemetry problem

25
00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:24.780
 Edge Microsoft's Chromium-based browser presents a different category of concern.

26
00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:29.180
 Independent analysis of Edge's network traffic has documented an extensive telemetry pipeline

27
00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:31.320
 that transmits browsing data to Microsoft servers.

28
00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:36.100
 This includes typed URLs even before the user presses enter, page content previews for the

29
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:40.740
 collections feature, and persistent identifiers that allow Microsoft to build longitudinal browsing

30
00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:41.320
 profiles.

31
00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:47.700
 Many of these transmissions occur regardless of the privacy settings a user has configured in the

32
00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:48.800
 browser's interface.

33
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:50.660
 What to do about it?

34
00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.700
 The practical alternatives are well established.

35
00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:59.880
 Firefox, maintained by the Mozilla Foundation, continues to support Manifest V2 extensions and has

36
00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:01.600
 committed to doing so indefinitely.

37
00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:07.640
 Brave, built on the Chromium engine but with aggressive privacy defaults, blocks trackers and ads at the browser

38
00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:09.880
 level without relying on extensions at all.

39
00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:17.420
 For users who require Chrome compatibility, configuring a DNS-level blocker such as NextDNS or PyHole

40
00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:21.320
 provides a layer of protection that operates below the browser entirely.

41
00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:22.820
 The tools exist.

42
00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:29.100
 The question is whether the inconvenience of switching is worth the privacy gained, and increasingly, the answer is yes.
